1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,180
It's quite hard to think of a city that doesn't have a big river running through it.

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:08,766
If you think about the major cities in the world,

3
00:00:08,766 --> 00:00:13,070
Shanghai, New York, Mumbai, London, they're nearly all built on rivers.

4
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:17,461
When these cities were established hundreds or even thousands of years ago,

5
00:00:17,461 --> 00:00:21,120
the rivers were a big part of people's lives.

6
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:27,360
In 16th-century London, the quickest way to get from one part of the city to another was by river.

7
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:32,194
But people also used the river for fishing, as the water then was relatively clean,

8
00:00:32,194 --> 00:00:37,350
and they would also go on boat trips up and down the river just for pleasure,

9
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:41,540
as a relaxing escape from the noise and bustle of the city streets.

10
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:47,430
But as industries developed and populations increased, city rivers suffered.

11
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:53,990
The rising number of people meant there was a huge increase in the amount of sewage discharged into the rivers.

12
00:00:53,990 --> 00:00:56,668
Rivers had always been used for this purpose,

13
00:00:56,668 --> 00:01:01,690
but when the number of inhabitants was so small that wasn't such a problem.

14
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:05,160
However, as cities grew to over a million inhabitants

15
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:08,740
the impact on the rivers became more serious.

16
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:14,894
In addition, other types of pollution increased as factories were built beside the river

17
00:01:14,894 --> 00:01:18,410
and discharged their waste materials into the water.

18
00:01:18,410 --> 00:01:20,550
This got worse over time.

19
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:29,504
As recently as 1957, scientists at London's Natural History Museum declared that the River Thames was 'dead' in biological terms,

20
00:01:29,504 --> 00:01:34,620
as the water was too filthy to support any kind of life.

21
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:38,528
But in recent years, as rivers lost their industrial function,

22
00:01:38,528 --> 00:01:44,780
cities have begun to recognise their true value and to take steps to clean them up.

23
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:50,440
For example, the River Thames is now cleaner than it's been for a hundred and fifty years.

24
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:53,500
These days you can see seals swimming in the water

25
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:57,028
and recently people had to try to rescue a whale

26
00:01:57,028 --> 00:02:01,640
which had got lost and swum up the river from the sea by mistake.

27
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:09,080
Unfortunately they didn't succeed, but the problem was disorientation rather than the quality of the water.

28
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,220
Then, all around the world

29
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:15,520
riverside areas are now seen as prime sites for development.

30
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:22,017
Warehouses that were once used for storing goods are now being converted into expensive restaurants

31
00:02:22,017 --> 00:02:24,740
and also into apartments with river views

32
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:29,380
which are in great demand and sell for astronomical prices.

33
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:32,920
In Los Angeles, on the west coast of the USA,

34
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:38,939
an architect has plans to revitalise the banks of the river and to make a park there

35
00:02:38,939 --> 00:02:44,250
which can provide facilities for sports as well as a natural environment for relaxing in.

36
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:49,261
It's also hoped that the riverside can be used for other purposes -

37
00:02:49,261 --> 00:02:59,670
it's been proposed that facilities could be provided for displaying projects related to various kinds of art that have been produced by local people, for example.

38
00:02:59,670 --> 00:03:04,345
In the city of Paris, during the summer months of July and August,

39
00:03:04,345 --> 00:03:09,380
all the traffic is banned from the roads by the sides of the river

40
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:10,800
and the banks are transformed into beaches

41
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:15,494
where people can relax in deckchairs under potted palm trees,

42
00:03:15,494 --> 00:03:19,810
sunbathe, or buy a drink or a snack while enjoying the view.

43
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:24,298
But to make the most of our rivers in our increasingly crowded cities,

44
00:03:24,298 --> 00:03:26,880
we need to allow them to regain their original purpose

45
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:30,513
and be used as a means of transport,

46
00:03:30,513 --> 00:03:32,930
reclaiming our streets from cars and lorries.

47
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:38,762
To do this we'll have to shift more traffic back to the river,

48
00:03:38,762 --> 00:03:43,760
but this time cleanly and silently, making the most of modern technology.

49
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:51,563
Already more than two billion passengers use the ferry to travel in cities around the world like Istanbul,

50
00:03:51,563 --> 00:03:53,730
San Francisco and New York.

51
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:56,660
And these numbers are set to rise further.

52
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:59,060
Admittedly, it's not a fast way of travelling

53
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:03,120
but neither is a car when it's stuck in traffic.

54
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:09,260
Of course, passenger traffic on roads might decrease as more people start working from home.

55
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:13,341
But another recent development, the huge rise in online shopping,

56
00:04:13,341 --> 00:04:19,870
has meant that another form of urban traffic just keeps on growing, and that's deliveries.

57
00:04:19,870 --> 00:04:25,360
Trucks and vans in the city pollute and double park while dropping off parcels.

58
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:31,000
Imagine using the immense capacity of shipping to take these trucks off the road.

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,080
One freight barge can replace 44 large trucks

60
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:39,390
uses far less energy and causes less pollution.

61
00:04:39,390 --> 00:04:42,159
When the barge docks at the riverside,

62
00:04:42,159 --> 00:04:50,070
the parcels could be taken the last few kilometres to their final destination on cargo bikes - electric ones, of course.

63
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:53,270
This is already happening in the Dutch city of Amsterdam.

64
00:04:53,270 --> 00:04:57,860
And in future, the final stage could even be carried out by drone

65
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:00,760
although at present this isn't allowed.

66
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:05,000
Wouldn't it be great to unblock our city centres in this way!

67
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,490
Looking further ahead...
